window.__labLoaded({"id":"5.3","title":"Confidence Intervals for a Proportion","goal":"Build 95% confidence intervals for a proportion with the Wald and Wilson methods, and see why Wilson is safer for small samples.","question":"A polling firm asked 850 voters and 476 support Candidate A. A clinic also tried a new procedure on 20 patients and 3 had a complication. We want an honest 95% interval for each proportion, and need to see which method (the simple Wald or the Wilson interval) can be trusted when the sample is small.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"A healthy poll: 476 of 850","text":"The lesson's poll. statsmodels' proportion_confint takes the count of successes, the sample size, and a method: 'normal' is the classic Wald interval, 'wilson' is the Wilson score interval.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_confint\n\nx, n = 476, 850\nprint(f'p-hat = {x / n:.3f}')\nfor method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n    low, high = proportion_confint(x, n, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n    print(f'{method:7s}: ({low:.4f}, {high:.4f})')","explain":["Load the proportion interval function from statsmodels.","Store the poll: 476 supporters out of 850 voters.","Print the sample proportion p-hat = x / n.","Loop over two methods: 'normal' is the Wald interval and 'wilson' is the Wilson interval.","Build the 95% interval (alpha = 0.05 means 5% in the tails) with this method.","Print the method name and its two limits."],"meaning":"The poll has p-hat = 0.560. The Wald interval is (0.5266, 0.5934) and the Wilson interval is (0.5264, 0.5930), practically identical. With a big sample and a mid-range proportion both methods work, and the whole interval is above 0.5.","out":"p-hat = 0.560\nnormal : (0.5266, 0.5934)\nwilson : (0.5264, 0.5930)","figs":[]},{"title":"A small sample: 3 of 20","text":"Now only 3 of 20 patients had a complication. n times p-hat is just 3, below the rule of 10, so the Wald interval is not trustworthy here. The raw Wald formula gives a lower limit below 0 (about -0.006), which is impossible, so statsmodels cuts it off at 0.","code":"x, n = 3, 20\nprint(f'p-hat = {x / n:.2f}')\nfor method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n    low, high = proportion_confint(x, n, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n    print(f'{method:7s}: ({low:.3f}, {high:.3f})')","explain":["Switch to the clinic data: 3 complications out of 20 patients.","Print p-hat.","Loop over the same two methods.","Build the 95% interval with each.","Print the limits."],"meaning":"p-hat = 0.15. Wald gives (0.000, 0.306): its raw lower limit is about -0.006, impossible for a proportion, so statsmodels cuts it at 0. Wilson gives (0.052, 0.360), staying inside 0 to 1. Here n*p-hat is only 3, so Wald is unreliable and Wilson is the safer choice.","out":"p-hat = 0.15\nnormal : (0.000, 0.306)\nwilson : (0.052, 0.360)","figs":[]},{"title":"No events at all: 0 of 20","text":"With zero events, Wald collapses to (0, 0), which claims we are certain the rate is zero after only 20 patients. Wilson gives a sensible upper limit.","code":"for method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n    low, high = proportion_confint(0, 20, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n    print(f'{method:7s}: ({low:.3f}, {high:.3f})')","explain":["Try the extreme case: loop over both methods.","Build the interval for zero complications in 20 patients.","Print the limits."],"meaning":"Wald gives (0.000, 0.000), claiming we are certain the complication rate is zero after only 20 patients, which is absurd. Wilson gives (0.000, 0.161), saying the true rate could plausibly be as high as 16%.","out":"normal : (0.000, 0.000)\nwilson : (0.000, 0.161)","figs":[]},{"title":"Coverage test","text":"Suppose the true rate is exactly 10% and n = 20. We simulate 20,000 samples, build both intervals each time and count how often they contain 10%. A good 95% interval should be close to 95%.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(3)\ncounts = rng.binomial(20, 0.10, size=20_000)\nfor method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n    low, high = proportion_confint(counts, 20, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n    covered = (low <= 0.10) & (0.10 <= high)\n    print(f'{method:7s}: covers the true 10% in {covered.mean():.1%} of samples')","explain":["Load NumPy for random numbers.","Create a seeded random generator.","Simulate 20,000 clinics of 20 patients each, with a true complication rate of exactly 10%.","Loop over the two methods.","Build an interval for each of the 20,000 samples at once.","Check which intervals contain the true 10%.","Print the share of samples whose interval covers the truth."],"meaning":"A 95% interval should catch the truth in 95% of samples. Wald catches the true 10% in only 87.5%, a hidden 7.5-point shortfall, while Wilson catches it in 95.7%. This is why Wilson is the better default for small samples.","out":"normal : covers the true 10% in 87.5% of samples\nwilson : covers the true 10% in 95.7% of samples","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"x <- 3; n <- 20; phat <- x / n\nme <- 1.96 * sqrt(phat * (1 - phat) / n)\nc(phat - me, phat + me)\nprop.test(x, n, correct = FALSE)$conf.int\nbinom.test(x, n)$conf.int\nprop.test(476, 850, correct = FALSE)$conf.int","expect":"Wald by hand (-0.006, 0.306), the same upper limit as Python; prop.test Wilson (0.0524, 0.3604); binom.test exact (0.0321, 0.3789); poll 476/850 Wilson (0.5264, 0.5930)","note":"prop.test with correct = FALSE gives the Wilson score interval (the default correct = TRUE adds a continuity correction and differs slightly). binom.test gives the exact interval. R has no built-in Wald interval, so the first lines compute it.","explain":["Clinic data: 3 of 20, and p-hat.","Wald margin of error by hand: 1.96 times the SE.","Print the Wald limits.","Wilson interval: prop.test with correct = FALSE.","Exact interval from binom.test.","Wilson interval for the poll (476 of 850)."],"read":"The Wald limits print about -0.006 and 0.306 (negative, impossible). The Wilson interval is 0.0524 to 0.3604, the exact interval 0.0321 to 0.3789, and the poll's Wilson interval 0.5264 to 0.5930."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (SPSS menus offer exact and Jeffreys intervals under Analyze > Nonparametric Tests > One Sample, but not Wald or Wilson)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / x n.\nBEGIN DATA\n3 20\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE phat = x / n.\nCOMPUTE z = IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE wlo = phat - z * SQRT(phat * (1 - phat) / n).\nCOMPUTE whi = phat + z * SQRT(phat * (1 - phat) / n).\nCOMPUTE ctr = (phat + z**2 / (2 * n)) / (1 + z**2 / n).\nCOMPUTE half = z * SQRT(phat * (1 - phat) / n + z**2 / (4 * n**2)) / (1 + z**2 / n).\nCOMPUTE slo = ctr - half.\nCOMPUTE shi = ctr + half.\nLIST VARIABLES=wlo whi slo shi.","note":"Both intervals are written out as formulas, so they match Python: Wald (-0.006, 0.306; Python shows 0.000 because it cuts at 0) and Wilson (0.052, 0.360). For the poll, change the data line to 476 850.","explain":["Declare the variables x (successes) and n.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","One row: 3 successes out of 20.","Marks the end of the data.","p-hat = x / n.","z* for 95%.","Wald lower limit.","Wald upper limit.","Wilson centre: (p-hat + z squared / 2n) / (1 + z squared / n).","Wilson half-width.","Wilson lower limit.","Wilson upper limit.","List all four limits."],"read":"The listing shows Wald lower -0.006 and upper 0.306 (negative, so impossible), and Wilson 0.052 and 0.360. For the poll change the data row to 476 850 and rerun."},"stata":{"code":"cii proportions 20 3, wald\ncii proportions 20 3, wilson\ncii proportions 20 3, exact\ncii proportions 850 476, wald\ncii proportions 850 476, wilson","note":"cii proportions takes the sample size first, then the number of successes. Wald may be shown as it is (even below 0 for 3 of 20), and Wilson matches Python.","explain":["Wald interval for 3 of 20 (cii proportions takes n first, then successes).","Wilson interval for the same data.","Exact interval for the same data.","Wald interval for the poll (476 of 850).","Wilson interval for the poll."],"read":"Look at the Lb and Ub (lower and upper bound) columns. For 3 of 20 Wilson gives about 0.052 to 0.360 and Exact about 0.032 to 0.379. For the poll both Wald and Wilson are near 0.527 to 0.593."},"conclusion":"The poll shows 56.0% support with a 95% interval of about 52.7% to 59.3%, so a majority is clearly in favour. The clinic's 3 of 20 gives a Wilson interval of 5.2% to 36.0%; the Wald interval wrongly extends below 0 and covers the truth only 87.5% of the time. Caution: small samples give wide intervals, whatever method you choose.","practice":{"scenario":"An online shop tested a new checkout page. Out of 40 visitors, 6 completed a purchase. Separately, 0 of 25 visitors used a new gift-wrap option. A third sample had 180 of 400 clicking a banner. Compare Wald and Wilson for all three.","data":"6 of 40, 0 of 25 and 180 of 400 (x successes, n visitors).","tasks":["Compute p-hat for each sample.","Compute the 95% Wald and Wilson intervals for each.","Where do the two methods differ most, and why?"],"hint":"Use proportion_confint(x, n, alpha=0.05, method='normal') and method='wilson' from statsmodels.","solution":{"code":"from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_confint\nfor x, n in ((6, 40), (0, 25), (180, 400)):\n    print(f'{x} of {n}: p-hat = {x / n:.3f}')\n    for method in ('normal', 'wilson'):\n        low, high = proportion_confint(x, n, alpha=0.05, method=method)\n        print(f'   {method:7s} ({low:.3f}, {high:.3f})')","answer":"For 6 of 40 (p-hat 0.150), Wald is (0.039, 0.261) and Wilson (0.071, 0.291). For 0 of 25, Wald collapses to (0.000, 0.000) while Wilson gives (0.000, 0.133). For 180 of 400 (0.450) they agree: (0.401, 0.499) and (0.402, 0.499). They differ when the sample is small or the rate is extreme, so use Wilson there.","out":"6 of 40: p-hat = 0.150\n   normal  (0.039, 0.261)\n   wilson  (0.071, 0.291)\n0 of 25: p-hat = 0.000\n   normal  (0.000, 0.000)\n   wilson  (0.000, 0.133)\n180 of 400: p-hat = 0.450\n   normal  (0.401, 0.499)\n   wilson  (0.402, 0.499)","figs":[]}}});
